<h2>How to Choose</h2>
<p><strong>If you publish several shorts a week from long videos:</strong> Submagic, and watch the credit split — 45 credits is about 5 Magic Clips but about 15 plain captioned videos, so a clipping-heavy workflow needs Pro.</p>
<p><strong>If you have zero budget:</strong> CapCut. It costs nothing, exports vertically, captions automatically, and there is no watermark on plain editing.</p>
<p><strong>If your source footage is messy:</strong> Descript first to clean and cut the long file, then a clipper if you want automated highlight selection.</p>
<p><strong>If you have an archive to mine:</strong> OpusClip's one-clip-a-day free plan is enough to test whether automated selection fits your content before paying $15.</p>
<p><strong>If you edit with other people:</strong> VEED for browser-based hand-off, or InVideo's free guest seats if the team works inside one project.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>What is the best AI video editor for Shorts?</h3>
<p>Submagic if you want captions and automatic clipping together — Starter is $19/month ($12 yearly) for about 15 captioned videos or 5 Magic Clips. CapCut if you want a genuinely free editor with a full timeline and auto-captions.</p>

<h3>Do I need a separate tool to make Shorts from long videos?</h3>
<p>No. Submagic, OpusClip and Descript all cut vertical clips from long recordings. Descript does it through transcript editing, OpusClip specialises in automated selection with a virality score, and Submagic charges Magic Clips per credit above plain captions.</p>

<h3>Is a free editor good enough?</h3>
<p>For cutting, captioning and vertical export, yes — CapCut's and VEED's free tiers both handle it. Paid tiers buy watermark removal, automatic clip selection and higher export resolution. Most creators upgrade when the watermark or the clip volume becomes the bottleneck, not before.</p>

<h3>Why do short-form editors bill in credits?</h3>
<p>Because the AI work is uneven — transcription is cheap, rendering animated captions plus b-roll is not. Two $19 plans can therefore deliver very different output, so convert credits to finished clips before subscribing.</p>

<h3>What export settings should I use?</h3>
<p>1080x1920 at 30fps is the safe baseline; 60fps helps motion-heavy footage. On the tools here, 4K or 60fps is usually the top tier only — Submagic's Business plan, for instance, is the sole tier with 4K60.</p>

<h2>How We Evaluate</h2>
<p>Tools are compared on published pricing, credit conversions and documented export limits. We do not present lab-style test results we did not produce.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scope:</strong> Editors built for vertical short-form output — captions, reframing, clipping and publishing.</li>
<li><strong>Method:</strong> Official pricing pages and documentation checked on the date shown above, plus vendor-published credit conversions.</li>
<li><strong>Criteria:</strong> Cost per finished clip, caption language coverage, export resolution and frame rate, free-tier usefulness, and how the tool handles long source footage.</li>
<li><strong>Corrections:</strong> Email admin@aizest.net with a source and we will update the page.</li>
</ul>

